# MCSP Hex Possibility Layer 0.44

Status: Constructive architectural extension
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Parent: CLOSED-ROUND-TRIP-0.43

## Placement

Hexadecimal representation is an explicit MCSP projection possibility before machine assembly/compilation and alongside disassembly.

Forward:

    semantic structure
    -> structural tuple
    -> HEX POSSIBILITY / ENCODING TABLE
    -> assembly / compilation realization
    -> machine bytes

Reverse:

    machine bytes
    -> disassembly
    -> HEX RECONSTRUCTION / TABLE VIEW
    -> structural tuple
    -> decompilation
    -> semantic structure

Hex is not semantic authority. It is a compact positional representation of binary machine possibilities.

## Hex table

Each hexadecimal digit represents four binary positions:

    HEX  BINARY
    0    0000
    1    0001
    2    0010
    3    0011
    4    0100
    5    0101
    6    0110
    7    0111
    8    1000
    9    1001
    A    1010
    B    1011
    C    1100
    D    1101
    E    1110
    F    1111

This is the complete single-hex-position possibility table: 16 determinate values over four binary positions.

## Byte composition

Two hex positions form one byte:

    high nibble x 16 + low nibble

Therefore each byte has 16 x 16 = 256 possibilities.

A three-byte machine encoding has 256^3 possible raw byte combinations before architectural validity constraints are applied.

The existence of a raw hexadecimal possibility does not imply a valid instruction or qualified semantic meaning.

## Qualified mappings

Existing 0.39-0.43 relation token 1:

    structural tuple {relation=1,width=64,dst=0,src=3,form=1}
    -> hex positions 4 8 | 3 1 | D 8
    -> bytes 48 31 D8

Second materially distinct relation candidate token 2:

    per-position inclusive relation:
      00->0
      01->1
      10->1
      11->1

For the same x86-64 width and operand locations its qualified candidate realization is:

    hex positions 4 8 | 0 9 | D 8
    -> bytes 48 09 D8

The mnemonic OR is documentary shorthand only.

## Disassembly requirement

MCSP disassembly SHALL retain both:
- raw byte/hex reconstruction;
- decoded structural projection where supported.

It SHALL NOT discard the raw hex evidence merely because a structural interpretation is available.

## Boundary

Hex representation is positional evidence and realization data.
It does not replace DETERMINATE CONDITION, semantic relation, provenance, applicability or qualification.

Kernel remains DETERMINATE CONDITION.
